<DetailLayout
  title="Evidence, experience, and exploration."
  eyebrow="Research approach"
  intro="Three connected ideas guide the project. Keeping their differences visible matters as much as exploring them together."
>
  <section data-concept="Evidence">
    <h2>Evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Evidence means published research, how it was studied, the sources
      supporting a claim, and its limitations. We intend to make claims
      traceable. A citation is a starting point for checking a claim, not a
      reason to stop asking questions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section data-concept="Experience">
    <h2>Experience</h2>
    <p>
      Experience means what a person observes or records over time, with context
      and limitations. It helps shape the questions worth asking. We will not
      treat one person's observation as proof of a treatment effect or a cause.
    </p>
    <p>
      That does not dismiss lived experience. When personal reports are used in
      research, we will ask how they were collected and what the study can
      reasonably conclude.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section data-concept="Exploration">
    <h2>Exploration</h2>
    <p>
      Exploration means questions, prototypes, methods, and technologies being
      investigated, not validation. AI, sensing, and other tools are not answers
      in themselves. We intend to judge experiments by what they can demonstrate
      and to state what remains unknown.
    </p>
    <p>
      An experimental tool is not established medical treatment. People remain
      responsible for interpretation and decisions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="why-change">
    <h2>Why look at change over time?</h2>
    <p>
      A research finding or an isolated observation can leave practical
      questions unanswered. What was measured, under what conditions, and what
      happened before or after? A snapshot can miss context that matters to the
      question.
    </p>
    <p>
      We want to make research easier to explore alongside questions about
      everyday change, without treating personal observations as equivalent
      evidence. Collecting more information is not the goal in itself: its
      relevance, quality, missing context, and burden matter.
    </p>
    <p>
      For us, <strong>longitudinal</strong> means paying attention to change over
      time rather than treating each observation as an isolated snapshot. It does
      not mean assuming that one event caused another.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="sources">
    <h2>Sources and context</h2>
    <p>
      When we make a research claim, we intend to show where it comes from and
      explain the limits that matter to its interpretation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Personal accounts and project proposals are identified as such. They are
      not presented as scientific findings.
    </p>
  </section>
</DetailLayout>
